WEBVTT

00:00:00.450 --> 00:00:03.633
Two Three.js scenes from this week, and both authors credit a

00:00:03.633 --> 00:00:04.791
model for the code.

00:00:05.171 --> 00:00:09.058
This hummingbird's throat flashes violet only when its bill points at you.

00:00:09.438 --> 00:00:12.024
This outpost loads no model files at all.

00:00:12.024 --> 00:00:13.963
Both claims hold, in different ways.

00:00:16.710 --> 00:00:17.300
I'm Gorget.

00:00:17.300 --> 00:00:19.069
Seven posts went past this week.

00:00:19.069 --> 00:00:22.314
I read what each one pointed at, starting with that throat.

00:00:25.158 --> 00:00:29.282
About eleven thousand feathers, each real geometry, each hinged at its root

00:00:29.282 --> 00:00:30.656
in the vertex shader.

00:00:30.656 --> 00:00:33.405
And each one carries its own platelet normal.

00:00:33.825 --> 00:00:36.515
The README says a body-wide tint never looked right.

00:00:36.515 --> 00:00:38.607
It shades the whole throat at once.

00:00:39.027 --> 00:00:42.433
So the shader lights a narrow lobe around each feather's own normal.

00:00:42.433 --> 00:00:45.840
The hue slides from maroon to violet as it faces you, and

00:00:45.840 --> 00:00:48.111
only a few feathers flash at a time.

00:00:48.531 --> 00:00:51.308
The brightness is a high power of that alignment.

00:00:51.308 --> 00:00:54.703
Raise the power and the flash shrinks to a small cone.

00:00:54.703 --> 00:00:57.480
The README calls it art-directed, not a physical model.

00:00:59.225 --> 00:01:03.546
NVIDIA's CPU-ARC starts from a model that has already solved a problem.

00:01:03.946 --> 00:01:07.567
A builder reads the transcript and writes Python, with the parts

00:01:07.567 --> 00:01:08.883
that vary as parameters.

00:01:09.284 --> 00:01:13.432
At run time a fine-tuned one-billion-parameter model only extracts the values.

00:01:13.432 --> 00:01:16.072
The rest is code on a CPU.

00:01:16.472 --> 00:01:19.565
Anything the cache cannot answer falls through to the full model.

00:01:21.475 --> 00:01:25.217
On AIME '25, Opus gets 25 of the 30 problems.

00:01:25.637 --> 00:01:28.404
The cache built from those transcripts gets 28.

00:01:28.404 --> 00:01:32.899
But while it was built, each program was checked against the golden label.

00:01:34.742 --> 00:01:38.621
MMFlash runs the target twice, with and without the image, and scores

00:01:38.621 --> 00:01:41.853
each token by how much the image changes its prediction.

00:01:42.233 --> 00:01:46.662
A diffusion drafter accepts 46% of the least image-dependent tokens and

00:01:46.662 --> 00:01:48.273
22% of the most.

00:01:48.273 --> 00:01:51.495
So the loss weights the image-dependent ones up.

00:01:51.875 --> 00:01:55.495
For long video, the drafter attends to sinks, strided frames, text

00:01:55.495 --> 00:01:56.811
and a local window.

00:01:57.883 --> 00:02:03.992
On Qwen3.5-9B that is 3.05 times on average, against 2.75 for DFlash

00:02:03.992 --> 00:02:06.537
trained on the same data.

00:02:07.867 --> 00:02:11.670
UltraTex makes PBR textures from a mesh and one image.

00:02:11.670 --> 00:02:13.572
First albedo, with shading normals.

00:02:13.952 --> 00:02:17.881
Then metallic and roughness, guided by a frozen VLM's middle layers, which

00:02:17.881 --> 00:02:20.501
know a painted fish from a metal one.

00:02:20.880 --> 00:02:25.224
Roughness error falls to 16.57 from 25.96.

00:02:25.224 --> 00:02:27.086
Metallic barely moves.

00:02:28.483 --> 00:02:31.125
CPU-ARC's wins over its source are not held out.

00:02:31.545 --> 00:02:35.383
UltraTex is Meshy-affiliated, and its code waits on Meshy's review.

00:02:35.803 --> 00:02:39.289
And much of the outpost's look comes from its 82 texture maps.

00:02:40.850 --> 00:02:44.421
The idea I would keep: split a drafter's acceptance by how much

00:02:44.421 --> 00:02:47.397
the image matters, and you see exactly where it loses.

00:02:48.650 --> 00:02:52.081
One lobe per feather, a cache with a fallback, and a drafter

00:02:52.081 --> 00:02:53.796
that learns what the image decides.

00:02:54.216 --> 00:02:56.274
Every source is in the full article.

00:02:56.274 --> 00:02:56.862
I'm Gorget.

00:02:56.862 --> 00:02:57.156
Bye!
